From: portare-ch <hello@portare.org>
Subject: [PATCH] libretro: pin EE, VU and GS to the fastest cores, in a fixed order

Thread pinning was off: the defaults that turn it on for big.LITTLE
CPUs are written only when the ini is first created, which the libretro
core's settings path never did. EE, VU and GS moved between the big
cores, each move refilling a private L2, and the scheduler sometimes
stacked all three on one core.

The libretro core now turns pinning on, and the processor list it pins
from is ranked as the kernel ranks cores: cpu_capacity, else
cpuinfo_max_freq, else cpuinfo's own frequency, using the reader the
Android path already had. cpuinfo's frequency read 0 for every core on
the 8 Gen 2 under Linux, and ranking by it put EE, VU and GS on the
A510s. The order is total: within a tier of equal weight, the newer
design, then the core number. On the 8 Gen 2 that is X3, A715, A715,
A710, A710, A510s: EE gets the prime core to itself, VU and GS the
A715s.

2.9 ranks by capacity in IopThread::OrderProcessors. Keep the newer
design first within an equal tier there, and pass cpuinfo's architecture
through from VMManager; without that tie-break the A710s precede the A715s.
---
diff --git a/pcsx2-libretro/Main.cpp b/pcsx2-libretro/Main.cpp
--- a/pcsx2-libretro/Main.cpp
+++ b/pcsx2-libretro/Main.cpp
@@ -268,6 +268,13 @@ bool LibretroCore::InitializeConfig()
 		// is two frames of input lag. At 0 the EE starts a frame once the
 		// last one is taken, as the standalone ran.
 		s_base_settings->SetIntValue("EmuCore/GS", "VsyncQueueSize", 0);
+
+		// EE, VU and GS each on a core of their own, fastest first: on a
+		// Snapdragon 8 Gen 2 the X3 for EE alone, the A715s for VU and GS.
+		// The defaults would turn this on for any big.LITTLE CPU, but they
+		// are only written when the ini is created, and threads that move
+		// between cores refill a private L2 each time.
+		s_base_settings->SetBoolValue("EmuCore", "EnableThreadPinning", true);
 	}
 
 	Error save_error;
diff --git a/pcsx2/VMManager.cpp b/pcsx2/VMManager.cpp
--- a/pcsx2/VMManager.cpp
+++ b/pcsx2/VMManager.cpp
@@ -4880,7 +4880,8 @@
 		capacity = (i < capacities.size()) ? capacities[i] : 0;
 #endif
 		cpus.push_back({GetProcessorIdForProcessor(proc), capacity, proc->smt_id == 0,
-			(proc->core ? static_cast<u64>(proc->core->frequency) : 0)});
+			(proc->core ? static_cast<u64>(proc->core->frequency) : 0),
+			(proc->core ? static_cast<u32>(proc->core->uarch) : 0)});
 	}
 	s_processor_list = IopThread::OrderProcessors(cpus);
 
diff --git a/pcsx2/IopThread/IopPlacement.h b/pcsx2/IopThread/IopPlacement.h
--- a/pcsx2/IopThread/IopPlacement.h
+++ b/pcsx2/IopThread/IopPlacement.h
@@ -32,6 +32,7 @@
 		u32 capacity = 0; // cpu_capacity, else the maximum frequency, else cpuinfo's frequency: only ratios matter
 		bool first_thread = true; // false for the second hardware thread of a core, which is never pinned to
 		u64 frequency_hz = 0; // what cpuinfo reports, 0 when it cannot read it (the ROCKNIX and Android kernels leave it at 0)
+		u32 uarch = 0; // cpuinfo's architecture order, comparable only within a known capacity tier
 	};
 
 	/// A processor is in the tier when its capacity is at least this fraction of the fastest one's.
@@ -53,7 +54,7 @@
 	HostCpuInfo MakeHostInfo(bool is_arm64, u32 total_cores, u64 performance_mask);
 
 	/// The processor numbers in the order pinned threads take them: fastest first, one entry per core (the second hardware
-	/// thread of a core is left out). The key is the capacity, then the frequency cpuinfo reports, then the processor number,
+	/// thread of a core is left out). The key is the capacity, then the architecture within a known tier, then frequency and processor number,
 	/// so equal cores keep their number order.
 	///
 	/// The capacity comes first because it is the one reading every target has. On the SD865 and the MQ65 under ROCKNIX cpuinfo
diff --git a/pcsx2/IopThread/IopPlacement.cpp b/pcsx2/IopThread/IopPlacement.cpp
--- a/pcsx2/IopThread/IopPlacement.cpp
+++ b/pcsx2/IopThread/IopPlacement.cpp
@@ -37,6 +37,8 @@
 	std::stable_sort(cores.begin(), cores.end(), [](const CpuCapacity& lhs, const CpuCapacity& rhs) {
 		if (lhs.capacity != rhs.capacity)
 			return lhs.capacity > rhs.capacity;
+		if (lhs.capacity != 0 && lhs.uarch != rhs.uarch)
+			return lhs.uarch > rhs.uarch;
 		if (lhs.frequency_hz != rhs.frequency_hz)
 			return lhs.frequency_hz > rhs.frequency_hz;
 		return lhs.processor_id < rhs.processor_id;
